WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
ARTHUR BROOKS: You
need to be bored.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.560
You will have less
meaning and you

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.778
will be more depressed
if you never are bored.

00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:07.070
I mean, it couldn't be clearer.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.680
Let me give you the good
side of boredom in general.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.600
Boredom is a tendency
for us to not be occupied

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.680
otherwise cognitively, which
switches over our thinking

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.980
system to use a
part of our brain

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.060
that's called the
default mode network.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:27.980
That sounds fancy.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:28.740
It's really not.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.198
The default mode
network is a bunch

00:00:30.198 --> 00:00:32.439
of structures in your
brain that switch

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.560
on when you don't have
anything else to think about.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.480
So you forgot your
phone and you're sitting

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.600
at a light, for example.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.480
That's when your default
mode network goes on.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.660
We don't like it.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.680
My colleague in the
psychology department

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.360
here at Harvard, Dan
Gilbert, he did experiments

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.840
where people had to
sit in a room for 15

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.060
minutes with instructions
to do absolutely nothing,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.120
and there was nothing
in the room to do,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.625
except there was a
button in front of them

00:00:57.625 --> 00:00:58.500
that they could push.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.042
And if they did,
they gave themselves

00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:02.020
a painful electric shock.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.260
Sit there bored, or get a shock.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.800
A big majority of
the participants

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.540
gave themselves shocks instead
of thinking about nothing.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:09.920
We don't like boredom.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.330
Boredom is boredom is terrible.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.710
Why is boredom so bad?

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.370
Well, because the default
mode network makes

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:18.290
us think about things that
might be kind of uncomfortable.

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.770
When you think about nothing
while your mind wanders

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.610
and thinks about, for example,
big questions of meaning

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:24.190
in your life.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:25.350
What does my life mean?

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.410
You go to uncomfortable
existential questions

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.690
when you're bored.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.633
That turns out to be incredibly
important, incredibly good.

00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:35.050
One of the reasons we have
such an explosion of depression

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:38.927
and anxiety in our society
today is because people actually

00:01:38.927 --> 00:01:40.510
don't know the meaning
of their lives,

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.430
much less so in
previous generations.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.770
Tons of data show this,
and furthermore, we're

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:46.830
not even looking.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.130
Why not?

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:49.330
I'll tell you why not.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.930
We figured out a way
to eliminate boredom.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:56.010
We've been able,
almost completely,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.170
to shut off the default
mode network in our brains.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.450
How?

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:03.490
The answer is that thing in
your pocket with the screen,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:06.250
which you take out even when
you're standing on the street

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.910
corner waiting for
the light to change,

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:12.078
is like, I might have to
wait here for 15 seconds.

00:02:12.078 --> 00:02:12.870
What are you doing?

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.495
You're actually trying to not be
bored because the default mode

00:02:15.495 --> 00:02:18.137
network is mildly uncomfortable,
because it sends you

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:20.720
to the types of questions that
you can't get your mind around.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.120
You can't get your arms around.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.740
Well, that's a big problem.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.300
That's a doom loop of meaning.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.580
If every time you're slightly
bored pull out your phone,

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.080
it's going to get harder and
harder for you to find meaning,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.380
and that's the recipe for
depression and anxiety

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.540
and a sense of hollowness,
which, by the way,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.820
are all through the roof.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:43.500
I get it.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.840
You don't want to be bored.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.340
You need to be bored.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:47.660
Be bored more.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.700
Tomorrow, when you go to
the gym in the morning

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.060
after you wake up,
don't take your phone.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.420
Can you handle it?

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.380
Not listening to a podcast
while you're working out.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:57.600
Just being in your head.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.340
I promise you, you'll have
your most interesting ideas

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.080
while you're working
out without devices.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.330
It's probably been a long
time since you've done that.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.060
Commute with nothing,
not even the radio.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:08.500
Can you do that?

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.860
Start getting better
at periods that

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.960
are 15 minutes and
longer of boredom,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.000
and watch your life change.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.580
Number one, you'll be less
bored with ordinary things

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:19.240
in your life.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.160
If you get better at
the skill of boredom,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.440
you'll be less
bored with your job.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.040
You'll be less bored
with your relationships.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.748
You'll be less bored
with the things that

00:03:25.748 --> 00:03:27.020
are going on around you.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.390
But more importantly,
you'll start digging

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:33.490
into the biggest questions in
your life, purpose, meaning,

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.990
coherence, significance.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:38.136
And who knows?

00:03:38.136 --> 00:03:40.922
You might just get happier.

00:03:40.922 --> 00:03:42.630
People ask me all the
time, is the doctor

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.590
taking his own prescriptions?

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:45.620
And the answer is, yes.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.730
Yes, I am.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.153
I'm prone to the same
pathologies as anybody else

00:03:49.153 --> 00:03:51.570
because I have the same brain
chemistry as everybody else.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:52.770
So what do I do to fight that?

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.812
And the answer is, well,
I do a number of things.

00:03:54.812 --> 00:03:59.910
I have a no device policy
after 7:00 in the afternoon.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:01.530
I don't sleep with my phone.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.070
We don't have devices when
we have meals in my family.

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:07.770
Because we're there
for each other.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.550
We're not there for
people who aren't there.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.790
Three, I have
regular social media

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.390
and screen cleanses where
I don't use my device

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:18.769
for longer periods of time.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:22.029
First, it's like children
screaming in my head

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:25.570
because that's how dopamine
is saying, get the phone,

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:26.470
get the phone.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:27.870
That's addiction.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.390
But it calms down
and I feel better.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.250
And I feel sort of
blessed by the end.

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:35.053
And I pick the
phone up by the end

00:04:35.053 --> 00:04:36.470
because I have to
check my emails.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.360
And I have to be a normal,
functioning, connected person

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.320
in the world.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.320
But it does remind me that my
life doesn't have to revolve

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:44.260
around these devices.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.680
These protocols are
really, really helpful

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.540
and I recommend them to
anybody and everybody.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:50.760
Don't sleep with your phone.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.320
No phones during meals.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.640
Regular social media fasts.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.830
You'll get better.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.580
People worry that if
they do these things,

00:04:58.580 --> 00:04:59.700
they're going to miss something.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.060
There are ways that
you can remedy that.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:02.435
One of the things
that you can do

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:03.810
is you can have your phone on.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.560
You're just not looking
at it, and there's

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:07.800
only one or two numbers
that can reach you

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.340
in case of emergencies.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.900
Phones can do that by the way.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.020
If you don't have to
do that, ask your kid.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.760
But don't use emergencies
in as an excuse.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.200
Here's something that's
not an emergency.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:19.860
What's going on Twitter.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:23.000
That's not an emergency
answer nothing.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.120
It doesn't matter.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.140
The news can wait.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.240
Seriously your
grandparents didn't

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.780
what was going on every single
second in Washington, DC.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.380
You're killing yourself
with this stuff.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:34.220
Are you kidding me?

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:35.760
It's bad for you.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.720
So let me say it
straight to my kids.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.400
Put down your phones.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.320
You need more
meaning in your life.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.090
And so do I.

